      "settings": {
        "eyebrow": "Luxury virtual staging",
        "heading": "Luxury Virtual Staging for High-End Listings",
        "lede": "Luxury virtual staging is high-end virtual staging for listing photos: a professional interior designer furnishes empty or cleared rooms in the image with furniture, art and lighting scaled to the architecture, so buyers of luxury properties can see how each room lives. Bella's designers stage Manhattan condos, co-ops, penthouses and townhouses, and luxury listings across the US and Canada, in 24 to 48 hours, from {price:virtual-staging} a photo.",
        "cta_label": "Stage my luxury listing — from {price:virtual-staging}",
        "cta_link": "/products/virtual-staging",
        "alt_label": "See our New York staging",
        "alt_link": "/pages/virtual-staging-new-york",
        "cred_text": "",
        "market_heading": "What luxury virtual staging is, and why luxury listings need a designer's hand",
        "market_text": "<p>Virtual staging adds digital furniture to real estate photos of empty rooms. Luxury virtual staging holds that virtual furniture to the standard of the home itself: pieces sized to the ceiling height, materials that sit naturally against the stone and millwork already in the photo, and light that falls the way the windows say it should. On a high-end listing the staged images are the first showing, and luxury buyers look at them closely. Virtual staging luxury listings takes a designer's eye; virtual staging for luxury homes that looks like a template does more harm than an empty room.</p><p><strong>Scale.</strong> A sofa that works in a starter condo disappears in a double-height great room. Our designers furnish grand rooms with pieces proportioned to them, from a sectional that anchors the living room to a primary suite that shows a king bed and a sitting area with space to spare, so potential buyers read the true size of the space instead of an empty floor. Empty rooms photograph smaller and colder than they are; well-staged real estate photos give every room a scale buyers can trust.</p><p><strong>Materials.</strong> Marble, walnut, brushed brass, bouclé and linen photograph as themselves only when the texture, reflection and shadow are right. Stylish furniture in the wrong finish fights the kitchen or the floors; the right finish makes the architecture look deliberate.</p><p><strong>Lighting.</strong> Floating furniture, a lamp with no shadow or a rug that ignores the perspective of the room are the tells of cheap staging. Every Bella room is composed by hand, and the designer will resize furniture until it sits in the room naturally, so shadows, reflections on polished floors and window light match the original photo.</p><p><strong>A buyer who notices details.</strong> Buyers at this price point have toured beautifully staged homes and scroll listing photos the way a collector reads a catalogue. Some are relocating or buying from abroad, and decide which homes to see from the photos alone. That is why luxury virtual staging is interior design work, not a filter: 83% of buyers' agents say staging makes it easier for a buyer to visualize the property as a future home (National Association of Realtors, 2025 Profile of Home Staging), and our clients use it because it helps listings sell faster and over asking. For real estate professionals, it is also a cost effective marketing tool on a high-end listing: the photos do the first showing for every buyer who finds the home online.</p>",
        "local_heading": "Luxury staging NYC: Manhattan condos, co-ops, penthouses and townhouses",
        "local_text": "<p>New York is where Bella does the most luxury work. Brown Harris Stevens' digital imaging team sends Bella new Manhattan and Brooklyn listings week after week, from Upper East Side co-ops and Sutton Place apartments to Brooklyn Heights brownstones, and gets designer-staged, MLS-ready images back, usually the next day.</p><p><strong>Condos.</strong> New-development condos sell the view and the light. Staging keeps sightlines to the glass open and uses low, contemporary pieces so the skyline stays the subject of the photo.</p><p><strong>Co-ops.</strong> Pre-war apartments have the proportions, mouldings and fireplaces buyers pay for, and furniture that respects them. Luxury home staging in Manhattan also means building logistics: physical staging needs a certificate of insurance, a service elevator booking and a crew that works within board rules. Virtual staging needs only the photos.</p><p><strong>Penthouses.</strong> Terraces are staged as outdoor rooms, with dining, lounge seating and planting, and double-height living rooms get furniture and art scaled to the volume.</p><p><strong>Townhouses.</strong> Each floor gets a purpose: a formal parlor floor, a family kitchen on the garden level, a primary suite that takes a whole floor. Staging tells buyers how the house lives from top to bottom.</p><p>Send the real estate photos your real estate photographer delivers, and every file comes back ready for the REBNY RLS and StreetEasy, with disclosure guidance. More on staging across the five boroughs is on our <a href=\"/pages/virtual-staging-new-york\">virtual staging New York</a> page.</p>",
        "hoods_label": "Luxury markets we serve",
        "hoods": "Upper East Side, Upper West Side, Tribeca, SoHo, West Village, Chelsea, Sutton Place, Brooklyn Heights, Beverly Hills, Bel Air"
      }
    },
